 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot K)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels (rays) and $K$ is the number of samples per ray [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for storing the volume data, where $V$ is the number of voxels, plus $O(K)$ per ray for temporary storage [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Direct visualization without geometric fitting; separation of shading and classification allows flexible material definition; handles semi-transparent data well [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Computationally expensive for high-resolution volumes due to $K$ samples per ray; interpolation artifacts if $K$ is too low; sensitive to transfer function design [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When accurate representation of internal structures and transparency is required, and computational resources allow for per-pixel ray casting [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time performance on very large volumes is critical without hardware acceleration; when geometric primitives are sufficient [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot K)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $K$ is the number of samples per ray [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$, where $V$ is the number of voxels, for storing the voxel database and intermediate arrays [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Performance depends heavily on the hardware and resolution. GPU implementations can achieve real-time rates for moderate resolutions [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Scales well with parallelization on GPUs [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Flexibility in shading and classification; independence of shading and classification allows for undistorted visualization [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Computationally expensive for high-resolution volumes or large $K$; requires careful handling of occlusion and depth [S3, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Suitable for high-quality offline rendering or real-time applications with moderate resolution requirements [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Not suitable for very large volumes with strict real-time constraints without optimization [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The volume rendering pipeline was formalized in the late 1980s [S3, unofficial]. A key early description was presented by Levoy at the SPIE Medical Imaging II conference in January 1988 [S3, unofficial]. The pipeline structure, distinguishing six stages (data traversal, interpolation, gradient computation, classification, shading, and compositing), is commonly referenced in literature such as Engel [S1, unofficial]. The term "CRIO" (Classification, Reflection, Illumination, Opacity) was used in parallel feed-forward algorithms to describe the conceptual treatment of samples as reflective, light-emitting clouds [S4, unofficial]. The pipeline assumes each sample acts as both an absorber and an emitter [S5, unofficial].
+The Volume Rendering Pipeline was introduced by Williams et al. in 1988, presenting a method for rendering surfaces from volume data without fitting geometric primitives [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. The pipeline was presented at the SPIE Medical Imaging II conference in January 1988 [S3, unofficial]. It has since become a foundational concept in volume rendering, influencing various implementations and optimizations [S1, unofficial]. The term "CRIO Process" (Classification, Reflection, Illumination, Opacity) was coined by Westover in 1991 to describe the feed-forward nature of the algorithm [S4, unofficial].
 
